# Purpose: Command-line flags for the chronyd daemon.
# Scope: Startup environment of chronyd.service (/etc/sysconfig/chronyd). It
#        is read through EnvironmentFile=-/etc/sysconfig/chronyd and used by
#        ExecStart as chronyd $OPTIONS.
# Dependencies: /var/lib/chrony/drift (for the -s flag).
# Standard: chronyd(8).
#
# -s   The main defense against the deadlock between DNS and time. At startup
#      it puts a floor under the system clock at the modification time of the
#      drift file, that is, the last time chronyd ran, and it does so before
#      any hostname is resolved. TLS certificate validity checks can then
#      succeed even after the RTC has died or been reset. This covers
#      chronyd's own NTS-KE connections and any TLS-based DNS transport that
#      the local resolver uses.
#
# -F 2 System call filter (seccomp) at level 2, which is a blocklist. It
#      blocks a small set of high-risk system calls such as fork and exec.
#      Unlike the level 1 allowlist, it cannot kill chronyd when libc or NSS
#      makes an unexpected but harmless system call, at the cost of a weaker
#      sandbox. Fedora shipped chrony 4.8-3 in October 2025 because -F 1
#      could kill chronyd when an NTS server was used with a newer glibc,
#      which is another reason to prefer -F 2 here.
OPTIONS="-s -F 2"
